---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const P1 = `count ← 0

REPEAT 4 TIMES
{
  count ← count + 3
}
DISPLAY(count)`;

const P2 = `x ← 20
steps ← 0

REPEAT UNTIL(x ≥ 10)
{
  x ← x + 1
  steps ← steps + 1
}
DISPLAY(steps)`;

const P3 = `local x = 20
local steps = 0

repeat
    x = x + 1
    steps = steps + 1
until x >= 10

print(steps)`;

const P4 = `for i = 1, 3 do
    print(i * 5)
end`;

const RefCard = `a ← expression           assign a copy of the result to a
DISPLAY(expression)      show the value, FOLLOWED BY A SPACE

=  ≠  >  <  ≥  ≤         comparison.  = means EQUALS
NOT / AND / OR           boolean operators

IF(condition)            no ELSE IF exists - nest an IF inside an ELSE
{ ... }
ELSE
{ ... }

REPEAT n TIMES           NO COUNTER VARIABLE IS PROVIDED
{ ... }                  if you need one, you make it yourself

REPEAT UNTIL(condition)  repeats UNTIL the condition is true
{ ... }                  THE CONDITION IS CHECKED BEFORE EACH PASS,
                         so the body can run ZERO times`;

const Compare = `while c do          -- checks BEFORE.  can run zero times
repeat ... until c  -- checks AFTER.   ALWAYS runs once
REPEAT UNTIL(c)     -- checks BEFORE.  can run zero times`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp-lua/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Pseudocode Challenge: Loops</h1>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>
            Four problems on iteration. The exam has{" "}
            <b>two loops and no <code>while</code></b>, and both of them behave
            in a way that a Lua habit gets wrong. This page is where you find
            that out on paper instead of in May.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Do this on paper.</b> There is nothing to run and nothing to
            type. On exam day you get this notation on a printed sheet and no
            computer, so practicing it any other way practices the wrong thing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="reference">
          <h2>Reference Card</h2>

          <p>
            Everything you need for this page. This is the same notation the
            College Board uses on the exam.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={RefCard}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Exam Notation — what you have so far"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Read the last four lines twice.</b>{" "}
            <code>REPEAT UNTIL</code> looks like Lua's{" "}
            <code>repeat … until</code> and it is not the same loop. Lua's runs
            the body and then checks. The exam's checks and then, maybe, runs
            the body.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Compare}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="Three loops, two behaviors"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <div class="alert alert-warning">
            <b>The exact Lua translation of <code>REPEAT UNTIL(c)</code> is{" "}
            <code>while not c do</code></b> &mdash; not{" "}
            <code>repeat … until c</code>. Same words, different loop.
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="problems">
          <h2>The Problems</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 1 &mdash; trace</h3>

          <p>What does this display?</p>

          <Code
            code={P1}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Problem 1"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            <b>Then answer this:</b> add a line inside the loop that displays
            which pass you are on &mdash; pass 1, pass 2, and so on. Say what
            you had to add <b>outside</b> the loop to make that possible, and
            why.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 2 &mdash; trace</h3>

          <p>
            Look at the starting value before you start counting. What does this
            display?
          </p>

          <Code
            code={P2}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Problem 2"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 3 &mdash; the same thing in Lua</h3>

          <p>
            Line for line, this is problem 2 written with Lua's{" "}
            <code>repeat</code>. <b>What does it print?</b>
          </p>

          <Code
            code={P3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="Problem 3"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Problems 2 and 3 do not have the same
            answer.</b> Say what the difference is in one sentence, then write
            the <code>while</code> loop that would give Lua the exam's answer
            instead.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 4 &mdash; translate</h3>

          <p>Rewrite this Lua loop in exam notation.</p>

          <Code
            code={P4}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="Problem 4"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            <b>Your version will be longer than three lines.</b>{" "}
            <code>REPEAT n TIMES</code> hands you no <code>i</code>, so if the
            body needs one, it is yours to create, yours to use, and yours to
            increase.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            This is the widest gap between Lua and the reference sheet in the
            whole quarter, and it is the reason exam questions about loops so
            often have a counter variable sitting above them doing nothing
            obvious.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your four answers, the added lines from problem 1, and the{" "}
            <code>while</code> loop from problem 3. For the traces, write the
            output exactly as it appears, including spacing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Worth <b>3 points</b>, graded on completion. Show your working for
            the traces &mdash; a wrong answer with visible reasoning is worth
            more to both of us than a right one with none.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
